# Writing Docusaurus Docs

Write for a person trying to get something done or understand how a part
of Replycant works. They are skimming, not reading the source.

## One page, one job

Pick one type per page. If a page needs two, split it and link.

- **Tutorial**: takes a newcomer to one working result. Ordered steps,
  no detours.
- **How-to guide**: solves one task for someone who knows the basics.
  Title starts with a verb: "Back up your library".
- **Reference**: formats, fields, flags, schemas. Complete and dry.
- **Explanation**: how a part works and why it is built that way.
  Architecture pages are this type.

## Open with the point

- Start with one or two sentences: what this is and why the reader
  cares. Never "This document describes...".
- List prerequisites before the first step.
- Common path first. Alternatives, edge cases, and internals after.

## Write plainly

- Address the reader as "you". Active voice, present tense.
- Short sentences, paragraphs of at most four sentences.
- Define a term or acronym on first use, or link to where it is defined.
- Cut filler: "simply", "just", "easy", "basically", "note that",
  "it is important to".
- Describe how things work now. Explain why a design exists, but leave
  change history ("previously", "we switched") to the changelog.

## Structure for skimming

- One `#` title per page. Sentence-case headings: "Clone without LFS
  filters", not "Clone Without LFS Filters".
- A reader should find their section from the headings alone. Stop
  at `###`.
- Numbered lists for steps done in order, one action per step. Bullets
  for everything else.
- Tables only for reference data whose cells are a few words. Rationale
  goes in prose.
- Bold for UI labels the reader taps: **Settings**, **Link a New
  Device**. Code formatting for commands, paths, flags, and values.

## Commands and examples

- Every command is copy-pasteable and works as written. Show the
  expected output when it lets the reader confirm success.
- Always set a language on code blocks. Add `title="path/to/file"` when
  the block is a file's contents.
- Use obvious placeholders like `<library-dir>` and `example.com`, and
  say what to substitute.
- One real example beats a paragraph of abstract description.

## Docusaurus specifics

- Set `sidebar_position` in front matter when page order matters.
- Link with relative paths to the `.md` file, e.g.
  `[Recovery keys](./encryption.md#recovery-keys)`, so the build catches
  broken links. Link text names the destination, never "here".
- Admonitions (`:::tip`, `:::note`, `:::warning`, `:::danger`) are rare:
  at most one or two per page. Use `:::warning` and `:::danger` only for
  data loss or security risk.
- Diagrams follow `diagrams.mdc`.

## Keep it short

- Cut what the reader does not need for the task or the concept. Detail
  only a code reviewer needs belongs in code comments or ADRs.
- Link to other pages instead of repeating them.
- No "Overview", "Introduction", "Summary", or "Related documentation"
  sections by default. Link inline where the reader needs it. End a
  tutorial with "Next steps" when there is an obvious next page.
- If a page needs a table of contents to navigate, split it.

## Before finishing

- Run `npm run build` in `docs/`. `onBrokenLinks` is `throw`, so broken
  links fail the build.
- Reread as a newcomer: could they follow this without opening the code?
